A||||9||Steven||Are you building a 60* V-6 (2.8,3.1,3.4)?||||1||
Z||000000||Steven||06-28-2001||01:48 PM||||A word of advise. Re-tighten the exhaust manifold bolt after you have about 100 miles on the car. Reason you ask? Well for me if I had done that, maybe by the time I got 650 miles on it, I would not have to go out and buy a new bolt and washer. Also you might look at lock washers. I used plain flat washers. I am going to switch them over (doing one bolt at a time so I don't upset the gasket) to lock washers this weekend. This is just my opinion. You should do what you feel is best.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Leadfoot||06-28-2001||02:20 PM||||I just put in a rebuilt 2.8 and used s.s. bolts with flat washers.  I think I'll do the same and change them to lock washers since the old bolts were kinda loose when I took off the manifolds.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Steven||06-28-2001||03:32 PM||||I used metric grade 10.9 which is equal to a grade 8 bolt. I looked a Stainless Steel, but when I called a place by me (Midstate Bolt and Screw) they told me that S.S. bolts are only equal to grade 5. I had a couple of bolts that had the heads break off over the first 125,000 miles, and did not want to try and drill them out ever again. So I went with strength over looks. Wish I could have both.||||reg||1
Z||000003||StuGood||06-28-2001||06:32 PM||||Very timely advice for me... I'll soon being reinstalling exhaust manifolds.  Thanks!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I tend to use Loc-Tite on bolts that might want to vibrate loose, but in this case I wonder if antiseize on threads, lockwashers under heads might be a better plan.    ||||reg||5
Z||000004||Steven||06-28-2001||06:38 PM||||I went with Anti-seize, and now lock washers. I don't want to have to drill and e-z out a broken bolt. <BR>By the way who ever name it an e-z out lied. It is never easy, and if your really unlucky, the e-z out brakes. Now I anti-seize almost everything when it goes back togather. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Leadfoot||06-28-2001||06:47 PM||||I bought the hardened s.s. bolts and not the standard ones so I think they may be a higher grade than a 5.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Steven:<BR><B>I used metric grade 10.9 which is equal to a grade 8 bolt. I looked a Stainless Steel, but when I called a place by me (Midstate Bolt and Screw) they told me that S.S. bolts are only equal to grade 5. I had a couple of bolts that had the heads break off over the first 125,000 miles, and did not want to try and drill them out ever again. So I went with strength over looks. Wish I could have both.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Steven||06-29-2001||01:13 AM||||You could call the people you bought them from. They should be able to tell you what grade they are equal to. And even if they are a grade 5. Who cares, as long as your happy. Besides it was the factory bolts that broke, and we all seem to know that those are junk. Maybe your s.s. bolt will stand up better than factory. I guess what I am tring to get across to you is as long as your happy with it, and are will to live with whatever happens. Good or bad.||||reg||1
Z||000007||chester||06-29-2001||01:34 PM||||When I pulled my old 2.8 I found one of the manifold bolts laying on the cradle, one totally gone and one that had the head rusted off.  Talk about your exhaust leak! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif">  When I put on my headers I used ARP stainless steel studs with stainless nuts that are collared and have these locking grooves on the collar.  Seems to be holding pretty good.  Got 3000 miles on the new setup and they were all tight.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||rc5star||06-29-2001||01:45 PM||||Great, I have to remove my exhaust manifolds this weekend and now my confidence, that it will go smoothly, is totally gone. I've been hitting the bolts with pb plaster for two weeks now so I guess I'll keep my fingers crossed. ||||reg||8
Z||000009||MoneypitGT||06-29-2001||09:57 PM||||Its not always that bad, I just did the back one on my GT that cracked completely in half, of course the week before inspection runs out....and amazingly it wasn't that bad. The car has 143,000 on it and there were no bolt casualties. I will say if I have to do one in my driveway from 11-3 in the afternoon on a 100 degree day on a black car ever again, I will pay someone whatever they want to do it.<BR>  Chris<P>------------------<BR><B>Check out my site at:</B>   <A HREF="http://www.customdynamix.com" TARGET=_blank>www.customdynamix.com</A> <BR>2 '87 GT Projects||||reg||7
